Were you ever in Germany?


00:06:06.283 --> 00:06:07.826
No, I have not.


00:06:07.993 --> 00:06:11.330
- Always been a regret of mine.
- Mom, you went to Germany.


00:06:11.497 --> 00:06:12.998
Remember?


00:06:13.165 --> 00:06:16.168
- Well, that must, um...
- Remember?


00:06:16.335 --> 00:06:18.504
Oh, must've been
a long time ago.


00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:22.633
Well, but you were there.
You loved it. Remember?


00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:26.261
The story of Alzheimer's
is never about one person.


00:06:26.428 --> 00:06:30.182
My PhD thesis film posits
that this insidious disease


00:06:30.349 --> 00:06:32.267
not only destroys the patient,


00:06:32.434 --> 00:06:34.144
but has
a physiological influence


00:06:34.311 --> 00:06:36.021
on the primary caregiver.


00:06:36.188 --> 00:06:39.316
<i>Alzheimer's occurs when</i>
<i>abnormal protein fragments</i>


00:06:39.483 --> 00:06:42.319
<i>accumulate in the hippocampus,</i>
<i>killing neurons.</i>


00:06:42.486 --> 00:06:45.406
<i>The disease then creeps towards</i>
<i>the front of the brain,</i>


00:06:45.572 --> 00:06:47.532
<i>wiping out neurons responsible</i>
<i>for logical thought</i>


00:06:47.616 --> 00:06:49.326
<i>and problem solving.</i>


00:06:49.493 --> 00:06:51.412
<i>It then assaults</i>
<i>the sensory region,</i>


00:06:51.578 --> 00:06:53.705
<i>sparking terrifying hallucinations.</i>


00:06:53.872 --> 00:06:58.293
<i>Eventually, it erases a person's</i>
<i>oldest and most precious memories.</i>


00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:01.755
<i>In end stage, Alzheimer's</i>
<i>destroys the part of the brain</i>


00:07:01.922 --> 00:07:03.799
<i>that regulates the heart</i>
<i>and breathing.</i>


00:07:03.966 --> 00:07:07.428
<i>When swallowing goes,</i>
<i>death is not far behind.</i>


00:07:07.594 --> 00:07:10.180
- Take this one off.
- Huh?


00:07:10.347 --> 00:07:12.683
- Well, it's so military.
- What do you think?


00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:14.768
Let's just... Yeah.


00:07:14.935 --> 00:07:17.104
Wouldn't you agree?
I mean, you always look so pretty.


00:07:17.271 --> 00:07:19.022
Whatever. OK, fine. Yep.


00:07:19.189 --> 00:07:20.732
- Thank you. That's great.
- Gone.


00:07:20.899 --> 00:07:22.401
- Happy?
- I am happy.


00:07:22.568 --> 00:07:24.278
Awesome.


00:07:24.445 --> 00:07:26.280
Is that
a shirt of your dad's?


00:07:26.447 --> 00:07:29.408
I guess I would have,
you know, a senior moment.


00:07:29.575 --> 00:07:32.494
Sure, you know, I'd put the toast in
and it would pop up


00:07:32.661 --> 00:07:36.039
and I'd put the... push it
back down again or something.


00:07:36.206 --> 00:07:37.916
Toast it twice. Big deal.


00:07:38.083 --> 00:07:40.335
On several occasions,
she left the stove on.


00:07:40.502 --> 00:07:42.754
OK? Burnt up the spice rack,
the whole wall.


00:07:42.921 --> 00:07:44.381
If you remember
your grandmother,


00:07:44.548 --> 00:07:46.008
she was frightening.


00:07:46.175 --> 00:07:48.093
She would leave a pot
on the stove


00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:50.721
until it melted right down
to the little glass top.


00:07:50.888 --> 00:07:52.764
- Maybe it's genetic.
- So that was a clue.


00:07:52.931 --> 00:07:54.641
I'm just saying what was a clue.


00:07:54.808 --> 00:07:57.227
Mia asked what's a clue for me,
and that was a clue.


00:07:57.394 --> 00:07:59.730
Alright. That was a clue.


00:07:59.897 --> 00:08:01.982
Deborah was exhibiting
what we call MCI -


00:08:02.149 --> 00:08:03.775
mild cognitive impairment.


00:08:03.942 --> 00:08:05.694
Through a battery of tests,


00:08:05.861 --> 00:08:08.197
we were able to diagnose
with 95% certainty


00:08:08.363 --> 00:08:11.200
that she was in the early stages
of Alzheimer's.


00:08:11.366 --> 00:08:14.203
How did she take the news?


00:08:14.369 --> 00:08:16.580
It's devastating,
obviously, for anyone,


00:08:16.747 --> 00:08:20.417
but Deborah's not someone
to go down without a fight.


00:08:20.584 --> 00:08:23.212
<i>After the premature death</i>
<i>of her husband, Dennis,</i>


00:08:23.378 --> 00:08:25.088
<i>to a pulmonary embolism,</i>


00:08:25.255 --> 00:08:26.965
<i>Deborah was forced to provide</i>
<i>for two-year-old Sarah</i>


00:08:27.132 --> 00:08:28.842
<i>on her own.</i>


00:08:29.009 --> 00:08:31.970
<i>She leveraged their house</i>
<i>as collateral and would go on</i>


00:08:32.137 --> 00:08:34.097
<i>to start a highly successful</i>
<i>switchboard answering service</i>


00:08:34.264 --> 00:08:36.016
<i>for the town of Exuma.</i>


00:08:36.183 --> 00:08:37.903
That was the way
that professionals


00:08:37.976 --> 00:08:39.353
received their messages


00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:42.731
and, I guess, transferred information
back in those days,


00:08:42.898 --> 00:08:44.658
was through
a professional answering service.


00:08:44.775 --> 00:08:46.818
Everyone told her
she was crazy at the time.


00:08:46.985 --> 00:08:48.505
- Right?
- They certainly did.


00:08:48.529 --> 00:08:50.030
I was all alone
with a small child.


00:08:50.197 --> 00:08:52.032
You can't tell Mom
not to do something


00:08:52.199 --> 00:08:54.319
'cause then she will absolutely
make sure she does it.


00:08:54.326 --> 00:08:56.245
- I was the nexus of this town.
- Yeah!


00:08:56.411 --> 00:08:59.331
Doctors, lawyers,
and Town Hall, everybody.


00:08:59.498 --> 00:09:02.918
Three-Martini lunches
and affairs


00:09:03.085 --> 00:09:05.754
and philanderers
and alcoholics and...


00:09:05.921 --> 00:09:08.131
I had to cover
for all of these people.


00:09:08.298 --> 00:09:10.175
You didn't have to.
You chose to.


00:09:10.342 --> 00:09:12.344
Well, I did or they wouldn't
have stayed with me.


00:09:12.511 --> 00:09:13.887
- True.
- I wouldn't have had a business.


00:09:14.054 --> 00:09:15.597
You know.


00:09:18.183 --> 00:09:20.852
<i>As the days pass,</i>
<i>what becomes most apparent</i>


00:09:21.019 --> 00:09:23.730
<i>is there are no small tasks</i>
<i>for an Alzheimer's patient.</i>


00:09:23.897 --> 00:09:27.109
- $27.96.
- 27?


00:09:27.276 --> 00:09:28.986
Let me see. Alright...


00:09:29.152 --> 00:09:31.822
OK, there's some of the change.


00:09:31.989 --> 00:09:33.949
- OK.
- I just need the dollars.


00:09:34.116 --> 00:09:35.826
Oh, right, OK.


00:09:35.993 --> 00:09:38.078
<i>Maintenance of daily living</i>
<i>exacts a great toll</i>


00:09:38.245 --> 00:09:41.373
<i>as organizational thinking</i>
<i>is compromised.</i>


00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:43.250
It didn't cook it
all the way through.


00:09:43.417 --> 00:09:45.335
I'm just gonna put this here
like this.


00:09:45.502 --> 00:09:47.754
- Why, thank you.
- Does it taste alright?


00:09:47.921 --> 00:09:50.799
- Is it sweet enough?
- Yeah, that is good.


00:09:50.966 --> 00:09:52.676
Can you taste the vanilla in it?


00:09:52.843 --> 00:09:54.845
- That's my secret.
- She's a fighter.


00:09:55.012 --> 00:09:58.390
And she's brave.
She's generous and kind.


00:09:58.557 --> 00:10:01.101
But how do you fight your way
through something you...


00:10:01.268 --> 00:10:03.604
..you can't see or know?


00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:07.190
I do all my little puzzles.
I do crosswords.


00:10:07.357 --> 00:10:09.109
I'm lifting weights.


00:10:09.276 --> 00:10:12.821
I am doing everything
that I have read will help


00:10:12.988 --> 00:10:15.324
to stave off the progression
of this disease.


00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:16.867
Stave it off!


00:10:17.034 --> 00:10:18.827
There's no cure.


00:10:18.994 --> 00:10:21.747
And so when I am
in the middle of something


00:10:21.913 --> 00:10:26.293
and suddenly my mind
just leaves the premises,


00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:30.631
there are no words to describe
how distressing it is.


00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:42.809
Debbie in the garden.


00:10:42.976 --> 00:10:45.312
Zoom in.


00:10:45.479 --> 00:10:48.398
Slow push.


00:10:48.565 --> 00:10:50.901
What is she...


00:10:52.319 --> 00:10:54.488
Luis, you're supposed to be
working on the edit!


00:10:54.655 --> 00:10:56.615
- Why are you screwing around?
- I was just shooting some...


00:10:56.782 --> 00:10:58.492
I'm hungry.


00:10:58.659 --> 00:11:00.786
- You hungry?
- Um...


00:11:00.952 --> 00:11:02.579
Let's get some lunch.


00:11:04.122 --> 00:11:06.500
Mia, lunch?


00:11:46.039 --> 00:11:48.542
Am I supposed to not talk?
Should I hold my breath?


00:11:50.419 --> 00:11:52.129
It doesn't matter, anyway,


00:11:52.295 --> 00:11:54.548
because, wow,
your blood pressure...


00:11:54.715 --> 00:11:56.633
- I mean...
- What?


00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:59.344
You know stress is
the real killer, right?


00:11:59.511 --> 00:12:01.346
Don't worry.


00:12:01.513 --> 00:12:04.641
I plan on dying of cirrhosis
of the liver, anyway, OK?


00:12:04.808 --> 00:12:06.601
- Right?
- Well...


00:12:06.768 --> 00:12:08.353
- Uh-huh?
- However you wanna go.


00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:09.938
Whoa, put that down!


00:12:10.105 --> 00:12:11.815
- Hey!
- Put down the knife!


00:12:11.982 --> 00:12:13.900
Hey, hey!
What, I didn't take anything.


00:12:14.067 --> 00:12:16.069
Look, whatever it is,
I'll help you find it.


00:12:16.236 --> 00:12:18.113
I'll help you...
Whoa, put that down!


00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:21.324
Hey, hey!


00:12:21.491 --> 00:12:23.493
Get away from me!
Where is my spade?!


00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:25.036
- I didn't take it.
- You took it!


00:12:25.203 --> 00:12:27.414
- Whoa, whoa, whoa!
- Holy shit.


00:12:27.581 --> 00:12:29.583
Tell her
I didn't take her damn spade!


00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:31.418
That's where it was!
You took it!


